---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const pillars = [
  {
    href: '/pillars/well-kill-methods',
    icon: '🧮',
    title: 'Well Kill Methods',
    desc: "Supervisor-level kill methods: Driller's Method, Wait and Weight, volumetric and lubricate-and-bleed, bullheading, and the ICP-to-FCP drillpipe schedule.",
  },
  {
    href: '/pillars/hydrostatic-pressure',
    icon: '💧',
    title: 'Hydrostatic Pressure',
    desc: 'The complete reference on hydrostatic pressure, the 0.052 constant, and how pore pressure, fracture pressure, and the mud weight window fit together.',
  },
  {
    href: '/pillars/rig-floor-leadership',
    icon: '🪜',
    title: 'Rig Floor Leadership',
    desc: 'The driller-to-toolpusher-to-OIM supervisory career ladder — what distinguishes each tier, and the certification that gates the step up.',
  },
  {
    href: '/pillars/stuck-pipe-and-fishing-operations',
    icon: '🎣',
    title: 'Stuck Pipe & Fishing Operations',
    desc: 'Differential sticking, mechanical sticking, jarring, free point and back-off, fishing tools, and when a sidetrack is the cheaper answer.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CollectionPage",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/pillars#webpage",
  "name": "RigFloorHQ Pillar Guides",
  "description": "In-depth supervisor- and engineer-level reference guides on well control, hydrostatic pressure, rig floor leadership, and stuck pipe operations.",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/pillars",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isPartOf": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#website" },
  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "ItemList",
    "itemListElement": pillars.map((p, i) => ({
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": i + 1,
      "url": `https://rigfloorhq.com${p.href}`,
      "name": p.title,
    })),
  },
  "author": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Pillar Guides — In-Depth Technical References"
  description="Supervisor and engineer-level reference guides: advanced well control, hydrostatic pressure, rig floor leadership, and stuck pipe & fishing operations."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Pillars', href: '/pillars' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/pillars"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/pillars" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">In-Depth References</div>
    <h1>Pillar <span>Guides</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">The four deep-dive references on this site — each one goes well past the hub pages into supervisor- and engineer-level detail on a single subject.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="sr-only">Pillar Guides</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      {pillars.map((p) => (
        <a class="card" href={p.href}>
          <span class="card-icon">{p.icon}</span>
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.desc}</p>
          <span class="card-link">Read the Guide →</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Scope</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">What Each Guide <span>Covers</span></h2>
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:820px;">
      <p>The hub pages elsewhere on this site (drilling, equipment, careers, calculators) answer one question each. A pillar guide is the next layer down: one subject followed all the way through, from the field-level basics to the decisions a driller, toolpusher or drilling engineer actually has to make, with links out to the calculators and shorter articles that cover each step in isolation.</p>

      <h3>Advanced Well Control</h3>
      <p><strong>Covers:</strong> pressure management once a well is shut in: why TVD rather than measured depth drives every pressure figure, pressure gradients, equivalent circulating density, and how the kill methods compare. <strong>Who it is for:</strong> drillers preparing for IWCF or IADC WellSharp at supervisor level, toolpushers, and engineers who sign off kill sheets. <strong>Start with:</strong> the <a href="/drilling/well-control">well control procedures hub</a> for shut-in basics, then the <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet">kill sheet calculator</a> and its <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet-horizontal">horizontal well additions</a>. Related: <a href="/blog/drillers-method-wait-and-weight-mixups">Driller's Method vs Wait and Weight mix-ups</a>, the <a href="/calculators/bullheading">bullheading calculator</a>, <a href="/calculators/gas-migration">gas migration calculator</a> and the <a href="/well-control-quiz">well control practice quiz</a>.</p>

      <h3>Hydrostatic Pressure</h3>
      <p><strong>Covers:</strong> the physics every other pressure calculation rests on: where the 0.052 constant comes from, how pore pressure, fracture pressure and mud weight define the operating window, and what happens at each edge of it. <strong>Who it is for:</strong> new hands and students who want the foundation once, properly, and anyone checking why a number looks wrong. <strong>Start with:</strong> the <a href="/calculators/hydrostatic">hydrostatic pressure calculator</a> and <a href="/blog/hydrostatic-pressure-0052-constant">where the 0.052 constant comes from</a>. Related: <a href="/drilling/mud-weight">mud weight and the mud weight window</a>, the <a href="/calculators/mud-weight-converter">mud weight converter</a>, <a href="/calculators/maasp">MAASP</a>, <a href="/calculators/kick-tolerance">kick tolerance</a>, <a href="/calculators/ecd">ECD</a> and <a href="/calculators/trip-margin">trip margin</a> calculators, and the <a href="/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet">drilling formulas cheat sheet</a>.</p>

      <h3>Rig Floor Leadership</h3>
      <p><strong>Covers:</strong> the supervisory career ladder above the drill floor: what changes between driller, toolpusher and OIM or rig manager, how the operator's company man fits beside that chain, and which certification gates each step. <strong>Who it is for:</strong> assistant drillers and drillers planning the next move, and anyone trying to understand who decides what on a rig. <strong>Start with:</strong> the <a href="/careers/driller">driller career guide</a>, then <a href="/careers/toolpusher">how to become a toolpusher</a> and the <a href="/careers/oim-rig-manager">OIM and rig manager guide</a>. Related: <a href="/careers/company-man">what a company man does</a>, <a href="/careers/certifications">oil rig certifications</a>, the <a href="/drilling/rig-move-plan">rig move plan</a> and the <a href="/blog/stop-card-system">stop card system</a> a supervisor has to keep alive.</p>

      <h3>Stuck Pipe and Fishing Operations</h3>
      <p><strong>Covers:</strong> telling differential sticking from mechanical sticking before acting, because the wrong first response makes either one worse; then jarring, free point and back-off, the fishing tools, and when a sidetrack is the cheaper answer. <strong>Who it is for:</strong> drillers and toolpushers on tour when the string stops moving, and workover crews running fishing jobs. <strong>Start with:</strong> <a href="/blog/differential-sticking-explained">differential sticking explained</a> and <a href="/blog/torque-and-drag-early-warning">torque and drag as an early warning</a>. Related: <a href="/blog/drilling-jars-explained">how drilling jars work</a>, the <a href="/equipment/hydraulic-jar">hydraulic jar</a>, the <a href="/equipment/power-swivel">power swivel used for fishing on workover rigs</a> and the <a href="/calculators/pipe-pull-margin">pipe pull margin calculator</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Where to Start</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Which Guide <span>to Read First</span></h2>
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:820px;">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Studying for a well control ticket:</strong> Hydrostatic Pressure first, then Advanced Well Control. The kill sheet arithmetic only makes sense once the 0.052 constant and the TVD rule are second nature.</li>
        <li><strong>Working on the floor and aiming for the console:</strong> Rig Floor Leadership for the ladder, then Stuck Pipe and Fishing, because a stuck string on your tour is the event most likely to test a new driller.</li>
        <li><strong>Engineer or company man:</strong> Advanced Well Control and Stuck Pipe and Fishing; both are written to the level of the decisions you sign off.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>None of these replaces your company's approved procedures or your well programme. They explain the reasoning behind them so the procedure makes sense when you are the one following it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
